※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=37 diag=none

※ `@[flag]` says an enumeration's values are meant to be combined.  What the
※ compiler chooses for a value that says no number is then the next power of
※ two rather than one past the last, so each value is a bit of its own, and the
※ bitwise operators `&`, `|`, `^` and `~` are defined on two values of the
※ type -- and on nothing else, arithmetic on one being a question about the
※ number, which is the one thing the type does not say.
※
※ A value of such a type may be a combination no single name stands for, so a
※ `match` over one needs an arm taking the rest.

@[flag]
enum Perm : u8 { read ; write ; run }

@[visible]
let mine: mut Perm = Perm.read

@[visible]
let readable: mut bool = false

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    mine ← mine | Perm.write
    readable ← (mine & Perm.read) = Perm.read
    let kept: u8 = match mine & Perm.write:
        write:
            32u8
        _:
            0u8
    ※ read is 1, write is 2, run is 4
    let both: u8 = match mine ^ Perm.read:
        write:
            4u8
        _:
            1u8
    let rest: u8 = match ~mine & Perm.run:
        run:
            1u8
        _:
            0u8
    kept + both + rest
